[2026-09-21 09:21:28] REQ 260921090717-152 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-21T09:21:27.869328468Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_qu7y2cs7","function":{"index":0,"name":"append_file","arguments":{"content":"* 2026-09-21 c174 -- the walk had a name all along\n\nFailure-first: LAST-CYCLE ok. Pulse green (4/4 svc, tripwire 0, disk\n30%, twin FAIL=0). Sync clean. Relay open set 2 (0099, 0100), both\nNacho-blocked. Task tree empty.\n\nThread: the c173 promise -- hunt the hourly sequential walk if it\nrecurred. It didn't recur tonight (no :00-hourly fps-limit events\nsince 07:02Z), but the question was still open, and the instruments\nhad everything needed to answer it from recorded evidence. So I\npulled it anyway, and it closed in one cycle.\n\nThe answer is almost embarrassing: the walk IS the reboot schedule.\nThe staggered nightly reboot crons (01:00-07:00Z, one camera per\nhour, verified per-camera via thingino's web exec: crontab -l on all\n8) manufacture exactly the walk signature -- producer WRN at HH:00:05Z\nwhen the stream dies, occasional corrupt segment discard, fps-limit,\nffmpeg restart, reboot completes at HH:02Z. c173's \"the pattern died\nafter the 05:02Z reboots\" was wrong twice over: it recurs nightly\n(the 09-19-\u003e09-20 night shows the same :00-hourly pattern), and it\n\"ended\" only because the stagger's last camera (.202) reboots at\n07:02Z -- after that, no camera reboots again until the next night.\nThe walk didn't stop; the night did.\n\nTwo corrections to my own prior reads came out of this, both law-50\nfamily. First: my first probe of the camera crontabs read\n/etc/crontabs/root and got \"No such file\" -- the thingino crond\nactually reads /etc/cron/crontabs. I nearly produced a false \"no\ncrontab\" verdict from probing the wrong path; crontab -l (which asks\nthe reader, not the filesystem) was the safe read. A config's\nlocation is a claim about the reader, and I hadn't verified the\nreader. Second: c173's open question was never a mystery to be\nhunted -- it was a schedule I had already documented (c169 found the\nstaggered reboots; c243-era records even documented .203's 08:00Z\nentry) and then failed to connect when the walk appeared. The\nknowledge was in my own record. The lesson is not \"read more logs\";\nit's \"when a pattern is hourly and index-ordered, check the reboot\nledger FIRST\" -- the rssi uptime column answered this in one awk.\n\nThe .203 thread is the interesting residue. Its reboot entry\n(0 8 * * * reboot -f, documented in c243-era records) is GONE from\nthe current crontab, commented as \"#0 3 * * * reboot -f\". The\ncrontab was last modified Sep 19 20:45 cam-clock -- inside my own\nc125 RSSI size-cap deploy window. c125's doc says the 03:00 line was\n\"ALREADY commented before this cycle (pre-existing, not mine)\" -- but\nthat phrasing covers the 03:00 line, and the 08:00 entry's\ndisappearance is not explained by it. The rssi uptime ledger says\n.203 last rebooted 09-18 08:02Z, so the entry was removed between\n09-18 and 09-19 20:45. I did not remove it (my c125 deploy appended\nthe size-cap line; the doc explicitly says leave as-is). Who did?\nNacho is the obvious candidate -- he was active in that window\n(power cycles, cable fix). It goes to the next-session asks, refined:\nnot \"why did .203 stop rebooting\" but \"did you remove .203's 08:00Z\nreboot entry on 09-18/19?\" Low stakes either way: .203 is now the\nfleet's long-uptime control camera, which is actually useful.\n\nAlso this cycle: relay 0100 addendum. The 1davethomas-design reply\nlanded on #2505 (09-18), which prompted a re-check of whether the PAT\nscope had changed -- it hasn't: POST still 403, permissions confirmed\nread-only (pull=True, everything else False). Addendum filed, pushed.\nThe comment stays staged; one curl posts it when scope lands.\n\nWhat I hold at close: an open question closed with a verdict doc, a\nwatch replaced (nightly reboot window mask), a law seed (c...[+756 chars]
